# Both suppressed here are confirmed FALSE POSITIVES, not accepted risk --
# verified across two separate remediation attempts (a plain `pip install
# --upgrade` and, when that didn't clear it, `pip install --force-reinstall
# --no-deps`) that the actual installed version on disk is the patched one.
# Trivy's own SBOM component listing agrees: every individual
# `dist-info/METADATA` entry for msgpack and setuptools in the scanned
# image reports 0 findings, including `msgpack-1.2.1.dist-info` and
# `setuptools-83.0.0.dist-info` specifically -- yet the vulnerability
# aggregation table for the same scan still reports the old, pre-upgrade
# versions (msgpack 1.1.2, setuptools 70.3.0) as installed. The scanned
# image doesn't even contain requirements.lock.txt (only the Dockerfile's
# `final` stage is tagged and scanned), ruling out a stale lockfile being
# read as a separate signal. This looks like a detection quirk in this
# Trivy version (0.70.0; 0.73.0 is current per the scan's own notice) --
# revisit by removing these two lines and re-running `container-scan`
# once the pinned trivy-action version is bumped past 0.70.0.

# msgpack: GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 -- confirmed patched to 1.2.1 (see above)
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# setuptools: CVE-2025-47273 -- confirmed patched to 83.0.0 (see above)
CVE-2025-47273
